A||N||16||TRL46202||Speaker help!||||1||
Z||000000||TRL46202||02-04-2000||02:57 PM||||I have a 87GT and the seats have the speakers in the headrest, so I don't think that they are original. All my speakers work and sound fine, but when I plug in the speakers in the headrests, with the harness that comes through the carpet on both sides just behind the seats, I hear the music but they make this high pitch whine and it does seem to increase and decrease with the reving of the engine. I cleaned the contacts, but that didn't help.<P>Thanks for any help!<BR>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000001||overlord||02-04-2000||04:21 PM||||those are not factory.if they make the noise.<P>whoever installed them probably forgot to put a noise reducer thingy a duddad.. sorry i cant remmeber what u call them.<P>i think it would be noise comiing from the alternator or something similar.<P>you would have to find where your radio is getting its power, grounds, and check those wires...you can buy a noise reducer thingy a duddad, or even better just reroute the wiring (he probably got power straight from the battery)<P>when i install stereos i make sure i get power from the fuse box..<P>this might help but someone might know exactly wht to do..||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula||02-04-2000||09:07 PM||||are the speakers amplified? <P>my amp did that to the rear speakers until I bought good rca's.<P>btw, it might be the dialkyldithiophosphate in your motor (word of the day)||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||02-04-2000||09:39 PM||||Most likely the power wires or the speaker wires are running to close to some other wire causing you to hear Electro magnetic interference from the ignition coil. Find the offending wire and move it.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-05-2000||02:47 AM||||GM cars use a "floating" ground principle. If its a stock GM radio the negative side of the speaker wires are shorted or connected to the chassis. This causes a "ground loop" with the radio. If it's an aftermarket radio the ground wire for the radio is hooked to the chassis. If an amplifier is used they may have grabbed a power wire near the amp. The amp needs to be hooked to the BATT of the fuse block or straight off the battery (with a seperate fuse). The actual whine you hear is caused by the alternator a high soure of electro-magnetic noise. One last thing to check is the ground strap that goes from the engine block to the chassis. It is located right back side of the engine to the firewall. It looks like a flat braided wire about 1/2 inch wide.<BR>I have put many stereo systems in cars and these are the most common problems for noise.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRL46202||02-05-2000||12:20 PM||||All the speakers and radio are stock. Just the speakers from the headrests are making the noise all tohers are fine.........||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||02-05-2000||01:33 PM||||Then trace out the wires I bet the negative side of the seat speakers is hooked to the chassis or the wiring in the seat is shorted.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||tiacPaul||02-07-2000||10:32 PM||||Can the speakers in the head rest be replaced?  If so what are the best kind?  Mine are still factory and sound like @$#% when i turn it up.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||12:06 AM||||Just a thought about the headrest speakers.  I rode in WT IIIs '85GT last weekend and could barely hear the speakers.  Turns out they were driving into my shoulders instead of my ears.  Think about this before anyone decides to spend $$ on new 3.5" speakers.  A alternative might be to find some '86 and up sail panels and install the rear speakers there.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||02-08-2000||02:31 AM||||The best replacement speakers I've heard are the Infinity Kappa 32.1cf. You can buy them from Crutchfield. The only other manufacturers of speakers are:<BR>Blaupunkt, Polk, Pioneer, Rockford Fosgate, and JBL. All are sold thru Crutchfield.||||reg||1
Z||000010||DJRice||02-08-2000||09:55 AM||||I havent check into this completely, but I hacked up the sail panels in my 84 because I wanted to mount the 4x6s up there, and it didnt appear to be hollowed out.  I didnt acutally remove the sail panel, but I cut a big hole in it.  I ended up building some goofy looking enclosures out of some electronics project boxes and mounting those over the holes I cut.  <P>Maybe someone who has dismantled the interior of an 84 can check to see if the speakers will fit in the sail panels?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Carrolles||02-08-2000||12:53 PM||||It sounds like you have the very rare much coveted AUDIBLE TACHOMETER option.<P>The headrest speakers aren't hard to get out        but they are an odd size (can't remember what size though).  You have to remove the seat cover.  If you remove the seat from the car it is easier to do.<P>I believe there is a zipper at the bottom of the seat back.  If so , un-zip it.  Remove the Hog Rings holding the seat cover to the metal frame.  Start to remove the seat back cover by pulling up on it.  You will note that there are a couple of metal rods on the front side of the seat that hold the cover in the bolsters.  Slide them out.  As you peel the cover up and off the seat you will have to remove another set of Hog Rings that hold the cover to the headrest.  Once these are out the cover comes off.<P>The speakers are just jambed insode holes in the foam padding of the headrest.<P>I had to rconnect a loose wire on one of my headrest speakers on my 85SE.  While I had the seat cover off I repaired the typical rip in the driver's side and swapped covers from driver side to passenger side and vice-versa to even out the wear pattern.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||02-08-2000||03:27 PM||||The speakers in the headrests are 3.5".  I just visited a stereo shop (I seem to have plenty of time these days) and they had a different take on what would be optimal in a Fiero.  4" separates in the dash (4" mids and tweeters), a sub, 2 amps (one for the sub and the other for the separates, and a good head unit.  He said that this has been used in competitions and won.  Only problem I see is the price.  A pair of 4" separates from Boston Acoustics runs about $400 and a set from Rockford Fosgate is about $300.  Alot of $$, but maybe what I'll do if I ever get the money (and a job).||||reg||1
Z||000013||fierosound||02-09-2000||01:30 AM||||There is a reason why separates cost money, and why 4x10's are cheap!  Usually, companies are producing HIGH QUALITY components.  With separates, you require a crossover to "separate" the signal to each speaker.<P>In my car, I have MB Quart 3 speaker separates - 1" tweeters, 4" mids and 5.5" mid-bass using KEF 3-way crossovers.  Additionally, I've installed a single 3" mid/tweet for a centre channel.  So I have 7 speakers creating the "front stage".  This gives you the effect of being "front row centre" at a stage concert.<P>In the rear stock location, I have Alpine 4x6 "plates".  These have a 3/4" tweeter and 3.5" mid separates with wired crossover, mounted to a 4x6 base plate.  This is more than adequate, because rear speakers are for "rear fill" anyways.  The volume level should only be enough to create the ambience of music being reflected off the back walls at a concert - you shouldn't be able to consiously hear them!<P>Unfortunately, nobody makes 4x10 "plate" speakers, so you have to build your own plate using separates for the dash.  This is what I did.<P>For more info, see my website:<P> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fierosound</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-09-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000014||fierosound||02-09-2000||03:13 AM||||There are many more manufacturers than just those listed above:  MB Quart, OZ Audio, Advent, Kicker, Eclipse, JL Audio, Clarion, Alpine, Polk Audio, Lanzar, SONY (but they usually sound "tinny") AND MANY MORE!  <P>If you're shopping at SEARS, Walmart or even Crutchfield, you're only scratching the surface on what's available.  You'd be best to pick up Car Audio, or Car Stereo Review - preferably a "Buyer's Guide" issue, usually Jan/Feb issues.<P>You can also check out the websites below.  They have info/links to several hundred car audio equipment manufacturers, retailers etc.<P> <A HREF="http://www.carstereos.org/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.carstereos.org/</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.mobileaudio.com/intdir/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mobileaudio.com/intdir/</A> <P><p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 02-09-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000015||Monkeyman||02-09-2000||07:34 AM||||I just went through the Crutchfield catalog and picked out a system based on the stereo stores recommendations.  I came up with almost $1900US!  I can't afford something like that!  I want a killer system but I don't need a competition system.  Now what.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Formula||02-10-2000||03:56 PM||||Heres my killer system.....<BR>Jensen CD player $150<BR>Jensen 300 watt triax amp $100<BR>Sony Xplode 10" $100<BR>Custom Box $50<BR>Radio Shack 4"x10" (2) $30<BR>Rockford Fosgate wiring $50<BR>$500 after tax and it sounds great.<BR>||||reg||1
